WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.737
Here is a short Java program.

00:00:02.737 --> 00:00:06.266
In main, sb refers to a new StringBuilder

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.283
holding the word "Hello".

00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:11.139
We print it, call change(sb)

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:12.820
and print it again.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.464
Inside change, the parameter text is given

00:00:16.543 --> 00:00:18.895
a brand-new StringBuilder, "Hi"

00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:21.170
and "world" is appended to it.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.021
So what do the two lines print?

00:00:24.021 --> 00:00:26.280
Pause the video and decide.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.997
To answer that, we need the simplest data

00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:35.599
structure with a rule: the 栈.

00:00:35.599 --> 00:00:38.660
Think of a pile of plates in a canteen.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.625
Clean plates go on the top: 1, 2, 3.

00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:44.596
The next customer takes

00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:46.404
a plate from the top

00:00:46.404 --> 00:00:49.690
and nobody pulls one out of the middle.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.588
The plate put on the pile

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.729
last is taken first.

00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:58.603
That is the rule: last in, first out

00:00:58.603 --> 00:00:59.520
or LIFO.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.340
A 栈 is a linear structure where elements

00:01:03.419 --> 00:01:06.416
are added and removed at one end only

00:01:06.416 --> 00:01:07.678
called the top.

00:01:07.678 --> 00:01:10.280
Push puts an element on the top.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.750
Pop removes the top element and returns it.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.270
Let's trace a sequence on an empty 栈.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.162
Push 'A', push 'B', push 'C'.

00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:24.210
Now 'C' is on top.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.295
Pop returns 'C'.

00:01:26.295 --> 00:01:29.889
Push 'D': it goes on top of 'B'.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:35.217
Pop returns 'D', and the next pop returns 'B'.

00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:37.200
Only 'A' is left.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.177
Notice that 'C' came out before 'D'

00:01:40.177 --> 00:01:42.148
even though 'D' is newer.

00:01:42.148 --> 00:01:45.350
'C' was popped before 'D' was ever pushed.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.769
Push and pop are the only

00:01:47.843 --> 00:01:49.535
ways to change a 栈.

00:01:49.535 --> 00:01:51.595
Everything else only looks.

00:01:51.668 --> 00:01:53.655
Top returns the top element

00:01:53.728 --> 00:01:55.200
without removing it.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.407
Java's library calls it peek.

00:01:57.407 --> 00:02:00.423
isEmpty asks whether the 栈 is empty,

00:02:00.423 --> 00:02:02.336
size counts the elements,

00:02:02.336 --> 00:02:04.690
and makeEmpty removes them all.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.481
Popping, or reading the top of

00:02:07.481 --> 00:02:11.025
an empty 栈 is an error called 下溢.

00:02:11.025 --> 00:02:13.623
Our implementations return null

00:02:13.702 --> 00:02:15.907
while Java's library classes

00:02:15.985 --> 00:02:17.481
throw an exception.

00:02:17.481 --> 00:02:20.080
Either way, check isEmpty first.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.856
A stack with a fixed capacity

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.621
can also overflow: a push onto a full stack.

00:02:26.621 --> 00:02:29.632
A dynamic-size stack grows instead

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.226
stored either in a linked list

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.150
or in a dynamic array.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.485
Now back to the puzzle.

00:02:36.485 --> 00:02:38.393
When a method is called

00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:40.071
Java creates a frame

00:02:40.071 --> 00:02:42.665
also called an activation record

00:02:42.665 --> 00:02:46.023
holding its parameters and local variables.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.693
The frames of all the methods that

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.288
have started but not yet returned

00:02:51.364 --> 00:02:52.890
form the call stack.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.004
Main's frame holds sb

00:02:55.004 --> 00:02:57.730
which refers to the Hello object.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.655
The call, change of sb

00:02:59.655 --> 00:03:01.820
pushes a new frame on top.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.504
Its parameter, text

00:03:03.584 --> 00:03:06.150
is a new variable holding a copy

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.074
of the reference in sb

00:03:08.074 --> 00:03:10.560
both refer to the same object.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.470
The first line of change makes text

00:03:14.571 --> 00:03:17.826
refer to a brand-new object, Hi.

00:03:17.826 --> 00:03:21.488
The append changes that new object

00:03:21.488 --> 00:03:22.810
to Hi world.

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:25.454
The object that sb refers

00:03:25.556 --> 00:03:27.590
to is never touched.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.729
When change returns

00:03:29.729 --> 00:03:33.040
its frame is popped and thrown away

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:35.903
together with text and the only

00:03:35.993 --> 00:03:37.961
reference to Hi world.

00:03:37.961 --> 00:03:40.914
Main carries on with its own sb

00:03:40.914 --> 00:03:42.077
still Hello.

00:03:42.077 --> 00:03:44.940
So both lines say: sb is Hello.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.921
If change had called append without

00:03:47.996 --> 00:03:49.725
reassigning text first

00:03:49.725 --> 00:03:51.755
main would see the change

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.935
because both variables would

00:03:54.011 --> 00:03:55.890
refer to the same object.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.221
Now let's build a stack.

00:03:58.221 --> 00:04:00.409
Each element lives in a node

00:04:00.482 --> 00:04:02.525
which also holds a reference

00:04:02.598 --> 00:04:03.838
to the next node.

00:04:03.911 --> 00:04:05.881
The top of the stack is the

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:07.631
first node of the list

00:04:07.704 --> 00:04:10.331
because the front of a singly linked

00:04:10.404 --> 00:04:13.394
list is the one place where we can insert

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.510
and delete in O(1) time.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:16.951
Like the slides

00:04:16.951 --> 00:04:19.064
we put a header node in front,

00:04:19.064 --> 00:04:21.519
a dummy node that holds no element.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.519
The field head always refers to the header,

00:04:24.519 --> 00:04:27.450
and head.next is the top of the stack,

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.700
or null when the stack is empty.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.829
This stack was made by push(11),

00:04:32.829 --> 00:04:35.687
push(22), push(33).

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.069
The newest element sits right

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:39.499
after the header.

00:04:39.499 --> 00:04:41.245
Thanks to the header,

00:04:41.245 --> 00:04:44.342
push and pop never change head itself,

00:04:44.342 --> 00:04:45.930
only head.next.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:47.529
Here is push.

00:04:47.529 --> 00:04:49.722
Let's replay the last push,

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:51.211
push(33),

00:04:51.211 --> 00:04:54.110
starting from 22 and 11.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.460
First, make a new node, tempNode,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.460
and store x, 33, in it.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.256
Then make the new node point to the old top:

00:05:04.256 --> 00:05:07.467
tempNode.next = head.next.

00:05:07.546 --> 00:05:09.974
Now both the header and the new

00:05:10.052 --> 00:05:12.010
node refer to 22.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.042
Finally,

00:05:13.042 --> 00:05:16.364
make the header point to the new node:

00:05:16.364 --> 00:05:19.091
head.next = tempNode.

00:05:19.091 --> 00:05:20.880
And add 1 to size.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.202
33 is the new top.

00:05:23.202 --> 00:05:26.231
Push touches a fixed number of references, whatever the size of the stack:

00:05:26.231 --> 00:05:29.190
O(1).

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.857
The order of those two link

00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:33.480
assignments matters.

00:05:33.557 --> 00:05:35.875
Suppose we write head.next

00:05:35.953 --> 00:05:37.730
= tempNode first.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.080
The only reference to the

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.000
old top is overwritten.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:45.600
Then tempNode.next = head.next makes the new node point to itself,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.480
and the rest of the stack

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.280
is lost.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.095
So, link the new node to the old top first,

00:05:55.095 --> 00:05:58.230
and only then point the header at it.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:05:59.569
Now pop.

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:01.750
If the stack is empty,

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:05.353
there is nothing to pop: return null,

00:06:05.353 --> 00:06:07.250
the underflow case.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.566
Otherwise, remember the first node:

00:06:10.566 --> 00:06:13.363
firstNode = head.next.

00:06:13.363 --> 00:06:15.906
Make the header skip over it:

00:06:15.906 --> 00:06:19.465
head.next = firstNode.next.

00:06:19.465 --> 00:06:21.499
Subtract 1 from size,

00:06:21.499 --> 00:06:24.720
and return its element, 33.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.398
This is exactly delete the first

00:06:27.471 --> 00:06:29.071
node of a linked list.

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:32.127
The unlinked node is no longer reachable

00:06:32.127 --> 00:06:35.110
so Java's garbage collector reclaims it.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.381
Top is even simpler.

00:06:37.381 --> 00:06:39.749
After the same empty check

00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:42.962
it returns head.next.element

00:06:42.962 --> 00:06:46.767
here 22, without unlinking anything.

00:06:46.767 --> 00:06:49.050
Both methods are O(1).

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.168
Our test program pushes 11

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.869
22 and 33 onto

00:06:54.951 --> 00:06:57.325
a new LinkedStack of Integer.

00:06:57.325 --> 00:06:58.880
Printed top first,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.073
the stack is 33, 22,

00:07:02.073 --> 00:07:03.710
11, size 3.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.288
Top gives 33.

00:07:06.288 --> 00:07:08.613
Pop gives 33,

00:07:08.613 --> 00:07:12.971
and the next pop 22, leaving 11,

00:07:12.971 --> 00:07:13.940
size 1.

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.828
One more pop gives 11.

00:07:16.828 --> 00:07:20.343
The next pop finds the stack empty,

00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:23.369
underflow, so it returns null.

00:07:23.369 --> 00:07:25.810
And isEmpty is now true.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:27.266
To sum up.

00:07:27.266 --> 00:07:30.860
One: A stack is touched only at its top,

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.613
LIFO (last in, first out).

00:07:32.613 --> 00:07:34.716
Push and pop change it,

00:07:34.716 --> 00:07:38.310
and popping an empty stack is underflow.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.636
Two: On a linked list,

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.971
the top is the first node

00:07:43.061 --> 00:07:44.857
after a header node,

00:07:44.857 --> 00:07:47.551
so push and pop are O(1)

00:07:47.551 --> 00:07:49.707
with no capacity limit.

00:07:49.707 --> 00:07:53.659
Three: The call stack is a stack of frames.

00:07:53.659 --> 00:07:57.700
A call pushes a frame, and a return pops it.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:07:59.830
Try this yourself.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:04.081
Start empty, then push(1), push(2), pop,

00:08:04.081 --> 00:08:07.740
push(3), push(4), pop, and top.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.090
What does top return, and what is the size?

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.290
In part two,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.911
we build the same stack on an array

00:08:15.911 --> 00:08:18.320
and make it grow when it is full.

